C# Type Conversion:-
------------------
What Is Type Conversion?
Type Conversion means changing one type of data into another.

Like:

Turning a string into a number

Turning a number into text

Turning a float into an int
example:-
-------
string → int

int → string

float → int

int → double

This is super important when dealing with:

User input

Calculations

Database values

File reading

2 Types of Conversion:-
---------------------


| Type              | Who does it?     | Safe?         | Example                         |
| ----------------- | ---------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Implicit          | C# automatically | Safe          | `int → long`                    |
| Explicit (Manual) | You do it        | May lose data | `float → int` or `string → int` |



1.Implicit Conversion (No data loss)-

C# does this automatically when there’s no risk of data loss


int num = 100;
long bigNum = num;  // No need to convert manually

Because long can safely store everything an int has.


 2. Explicit Conversion (Done Manually):-
 
-You must convert it manually
-May cause data loss or error
-There are 3 main ways:

A. Using Convert Class-

string ageText = "25";
int age = Convert.ToInt32(ageText);

 It reads the text "25" and converts it into number 25.
 
Other Convert Methods:
Convert.ToDouble()

Convert.ToBoolean()

Convert.ToString()


B. Using .ToString() — For Converting Any Value to String-

Example:-

int marks = 90;
string markText = marks.ToString();

Now you can store or print it like text.


C. Using Type Casting — (type):

Example:-

double height = 5.8;
int roundedHeight = (int)height;
Console.WriteLine(roundedHeight);  // Output: 5

It drops the decimal part.


D. Using Parse() – Convert from String to Number:

string salary = "50000";
int sal = int.Parse(salary);

Works only if the string is pure number.


E.Using TryParse() – Safer Way (No Error)-

TryParse() is a safe way to convert a string to a number without crashing the program.

It tells you:

*if the conversion succeeded

*or failed (without throwing an error)

Why Use TryParse() Instead of Parse()?

| Feature           | `Parse()`                | `TryParse()`                      |
| ----------------- | ------------------------ | --------------------------------- |
| Crashes on error? | Yes (throws exception)   |  No (returns `false`)            |
| Safe to use?      | Risky for bad input      |  Best for user input or unknown  |
| Returns what?     | The converted value      | `true` or `false` + output result |



. Syntax of TryParse()-

bool result = int.TryParse(stringValue, out int variableName);

| Part               | Meaning                                              |
| ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| `stringValue`      | The value to convert (usually user input)            |
| `out int variable` | The output variable to store the number              |
| `bool result`      | Will be `true` if conversion succeeded, else `false` |

Simple Working Example-
-------------------------

string input = "123";
int number;

bool success = int.TryParse(input, out number);

if (success)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Conversion successful: " + number);
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Invalid number input.");
}


Explanation:
"123" is a valid number

TryParse() successfully converts it into int

number = 123

success = true


Example with Invalid Input-
-------------------------

string input = "abc123";
int number;

bool success = int.TryParse(input, out number);

if (success)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Conversion successful: " + number);
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("Invalid number input.");  // This will run
}


Output:-

Invalid number input.

"abc123" can’t be converted to a number — so TryParse() fails gracefully without crashing.



Final Real-Time Example:-


using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.Write("Enter your age: ");
        string input = Console.ReadLine();

        int age;
        if (int.TryParse(input, out age))
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Your age is: " + age);
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Please enter a valid number for age.");
        }
    }
}


Safe and user-friendly — works even if user types wrong values.


Summary Table-

| Task                        | Use This                              |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Convert string → int safely | `int.TryParse("25", out int x)`       |
| Check if input is valid     | It returns `true` or `false`          |
| Avoid crashing on bad input | Use `TryParse()` instead of `Parse()` |




Summary Table-

| From → To      | Method/Code                  | Notes                                     |
| -------------- | ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| `int → string` | `int x = 10; x.ToString();`  | For printing or joining with text         |
| `string → int` | `Convert.ToInt32("25")`      | Converts clean number in string           |
| `string → int` | `int.Parse("40")`            | Same as above but throws error if invalid |
| `string → int` | `int.TryParse("abc", out x)` | Safer – returns false on error            |
| `float → int`  | `(int)5.9`                   | Drops decimals                            |




